[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says: one issue, the parser, fix queued. The reports say more.
Read by what each one shows, not by its subject line, they split into three problems.
Each has its own ticket now.

<Callout kind="warn">
Fixing the parser closes only the first row. The other two keep coming after the fix ships.
</Callout>

## The split

<ProblemSplit ticket={4471} />

## The three problems

**`parser`: the shared parser reads every date month first.** Our tenants write the day first,
so 7 April comes out as 4 July. Every report shows a day and a month swapped. It reaches the
desk from the app, by email and through `booking-v2` forms. This is the bug #4471 describes.

**`hint`: `booking-v3` prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field.** Tenants on `booking-v3` are
day-first. Their people type what the hint tells them, month first, and the form reads it the
tenant's way. The result looks the same as `parser`, a swap, but the parser fix does not touch
it: the hint is wrong. Only reports sent through a `booking-v3` form. Intake tags these by the
reporter's browser (`en-US`), which is a guess: it misses some and tags others that are not.

**`shift`: the booking moves one day back.** Tuesday 12 May shows as Monday 11 May. The
month stays. One meeting lands on Tuesday in Lisbon and Monday in Toronto; an invite arrives
before the booking was made. That is a timezone problem, not a date-order one, and no date
parser fix reaches it. The desk already noted these reports as a one-day move
(`desk-notes.json`), and the #4471 table filed them under `parser` by tenant anyway.

## One report from each

<ProblemSplit ticket={4471} examples />

## Why one ticket hid three

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on the subject line alone.
Every subject here is some version of "the date is wrong", so every report landed in one ticket. The
subject tells you the symptom; the body, the tenant's locale and its booking form tell you the
cause. The table above runs `causes.ts` on every build, plus the desk's one-day-move notes, so
it stays right as more reports are merged.

## What to do

- Engineering: three fixes, three tickets. Ship the parser fix, then check the `hint` and `shift`
  rows still grow; they should, until their own fixes land.
- Desk: until then, triage a new "wrong day" report by its body and tenant, not its subject.
  A swap through a `booking-v3` form is `hint`; a one-day move is `shift`; any other swap from a
  day-first tenant is `parser`.
- 0001 stays as it was accepted. Replacing it is a new decision record.

